{
  "dom_id": "step-q142467",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Solve the equation $2{log}_{5}⁡(2x)-{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}+1)={log}_{5}⁡3.$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the logarithms share the same base.\n\nFirst, we apply the power rule to the *first* term to make the coefficient in front of each logarithm the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{5}⁡(2x)-{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}+1) & ={log}_{5}⁡3 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(4{x}^{2})-{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}+1) & ={log}_{5}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we combine the logarithms on the left-hand side using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}) & ={log}_{5}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the equation for $x$ using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =3 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}} & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =3{x}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ or $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{5}⁡(2\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}))-{log}_{5}⁡((-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}⁡3 \\\\ 2{log}_{5}⁡(-2\\sqrt{3})-{log}_{5}⁡(4) & \\ne {log}_{5}⁡3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause ${log}_{5}⁡(-2\\sqrt{3})$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{5}⁡(2\\cdot \\sqrt{3})-{log}_{5}⁡((\\sqrt{3})^{2}+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}⁡3 \\\\ 2{log}_{5}⁡(2\\sqrt{3})-{log}_{5}⁡(4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}⁡3 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡((2\\sqrt{3})^{2})-{log}_{5}⁡(4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}⁡3 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(12)-{log}_{5}⁡(4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}⁡3 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{12}{4}) & ={log}_{5}⁡3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\sqrt{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=\\sqrt{3}$ only\".",
